 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Precision**: Half-precision training requires loss scaling to prevent underflow [S2].
-*   **Boundaries**: The default scene extent calculation is biased by camera distribution; use the corrected extent based on SfM points [S4].
-*   **Degenerate Input**: If cameras are clustered (e.g., orbiting a small object), the default scene extent will be too small, leading to incorrect densification [S4].
+*   **Scene Extent Bias:** Using camera positions to define scene extent can lead to incorrect splitting/pruning if cameras are clustered (e.g., orbiting a small object) [S4]. Use SfM points for extent calculation [S4].
+*   **Half-Precision Stability:** Training in FP16 requires an automatic scaling factor for loss values to prevent gradient underflow [S2].
+*   **Register Pressure:** Fetching feature vectors in half-precision during rasterization reduces register pressure and improves performance [S2].
+*   **Convergence:** Training does not stop at a specific loss threshold but runs for a fixed iteration budget (e.g., 30,000) [S9, unofficial].
 
