+### Corrected Scene-Extent
+A method to calculate scene extent based on the SfM point cloud rather than camera positions to avoid bias from camera distribution (e.g., orbiting a small object in a large environment) [S4]. The corrected scene extent is $e'_{\text{scene}} = \frac{1}{N_{\text{SfM}}} \sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{SfM}}} \|\bar{C} - p_i\|_2$, where $\bar{C}$ is the average camera position and $p_i$ are SfM points [S4]. The default method using camera positions can lead to incorrect splitting/pruning if cameras are clustered [S4].
+
+### Selective Defocus Blurring
+Predicts a per-Gaussian scale offset $δs_j$ to selectively enlarge covariances, creating pixel-wise variable blur kernels that match spatially varying scene blurriness [S5]. A compact MLP predicts $δs_j$ for each Gaussian, which is added to the base scale before projection [S5]. If $δs_j$ is constant, it degrades to a uniform Gaussian blur [S5].
+
+### Denoise-GS
+A self-supervised denoising method for sparse-view 3D Gaussian Splatting that combines unsupervised image denoising with explicitly differentiable 3DGS [S6]. It uses Noise2Void (N2V) to train a UNet for denoising, processes denoised images through InstantSplat for initialization, and fine-tunes the UNet in a second round [S6]. Unlike supervised methods, it requires no paired clean data [S6].
+
+### Multi-Scale Constraint
+A technique using position-aware point adaptive control to apply different scales to Gaussians based on viewer distance, improving rendering of distant objects [S7]. It uses a scaling factor $\gamma(\mu)$ based on distance from camera center $c$ and scene radius $r$ [S7]. Cloning occurs if $\max(s) < g \cdot \gamma(\mu)$, splitting if $\max(s) \ge g \cdot \gamma(\mu)$, and elimination if $\max(s) > b \cdot \gamma(\mu)$ [S7].
+
+### LS-Gaussian Viewpoint Transformation
+An algorithm that leverages inter-frame continuity for efficient sparse rendering by transforming the viewpoint between consecutive frames, rather than rendering each frame separately [S8]. It operates at the tile/Gaussian level to enable sparse rendering, used in LS-Gaussian to render only one frame in every 6 frames [S8]. Rendering quality degrades as the number of consecutively transformed frames increases due to interpolation error accumulation [S8].
+
+### 3D Gaussian Splatting Training Loop
+Per-image error compares one render against one photo. Scene-wide error is the loss averaged across all training views [S9, unofficial]. The loop picks one input photo, renders from its known pose, computes error (L1 + D-SSIM, $\lambda \approx 0.2$), and updates Gaussians via gradient descent [S9, unofficial]. Training runs for a fixed iteration budget (e.g., 30,000) [S9, unofficial].
+
+### Camera Localisation for Convergence Basin
+Localizes camera pose by minimizing photometric error against the ground-truth colour image of the target view, fixing $G_{init}$ during optimization [S10, unofficial]. The estimated pose $T_{CW}^{est}$ is found by minimizing $\| I(\mathcal{G}_{init}, T_{CW}) - \bar{I}_{target} \|_1$ [S10, unofficial].
+
+### DiffGS Framework
+Applies an optimization process during Gaussian extraction to optimize Gaussians to exact locations [S11]. Uses a truncation function $\lambda$ and projection function $\tau$ (e.g., $\tau(x) = e^{-x}$) [S11]. 350K Gaussians achieve PSNR 34.01, SSIM 0.9879, LPIPS 0.0149 [S11].
+
+### Opacity Offset Table for DSA
+Constructs an opacity offset table to model changes in digital subtraction angiography [S12].
+
+### Resolution Guided Primitive Scheduler
+Updates primitive budget $P_{fin}^{(i)} = \max(P_{fin}^{(i-1)}, \gamma \cdot P_{fin}^{(i-1)} + \eta \cdot P_{add})$ [S13]. Power factor starts at 2 and decreases to 1. $\gamma=0.98$, $\eta=1$ [S13]. Too much growth at the end causes poor convergence [S13].
+
+### Conditional Splat Approximation
+Approximates the influence range of a 3D Gaussian on a slice by using a 2D ellipse splat scaled by the marginal factor, closely matching true iso-probability contours [S14, unofficial].
+
+### Deformation-Aware Pruning
+Identifies and removes redundant Gaussians by gauging their impact on deformation, distinguishing between stable and deformed Gaussians [S15].
+
+### GEMM-GS
+An acceleration approach utilizing Tensor Cores by reformulating the blending process into a GEMM-compatible form [S16]. Uses a three-stage double-buffered CUDA kernel pipeline [S16]. Achieves 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS [S16].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 
-### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Time**: The training time is dominated by the differentiable rasterization and gradient computation, which scales linearly with the number of Gaussians and pixels [S9, unofficial]. The GEMM-GS acceleration provides a 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS [S16].
-*   **Memory**: Memory usage scales with the number of Gaussians and their attributes. For SH degree 1, each Gaussian requires 3 (pos) + 4 (rot) + 3 (scale) + 1 (opacity) + 12 (color) = 23 floats. For millions of Gaussians, this can exceed 1GB [12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md].
-
-### Measured Performance
-*   **Hardware**: Tested on GPUs with Tensor Cores (e.g., NVIDIA A100, RTX 4090) [S16].
-*   **Resolution**: Performance is measured at various resolutions, with 16x16 tiles being a common block size for parallelization [S16].
-*   **Scene**: Tested on standard datasets like Mip-NeRF 360 and Tanks & Temples [S4, S16].
-
-### Scaling
-*   **Large Scenes**: Methods like TraGraph-GS and Distributed Training address scalability to large scenes by partitioning the scene or sharding the Gaussian population [S7, S15108-distributed-training-and-sharding.md].
-*   **High Resolution**: Frequency-guided resolution schedulers and error-guided sparse sampling help manage the computational cost of high-resolution rendering [S15751-frequency-guided-resolution-scheduler.md, S15708-error-guided-sparse-pixel-sampling.md].
-
-### Strengths and Limitations
-*   **Strengths**: Real-time rendering, high fidelity, explicit representation [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: High memory usage, sensitivity to initialization, potential for artifacts in sparse-view scenarios [S6, S12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md].
-
-### When to Use
-*   Use when real-time rendering and high visual fidelity are required.
-*   Use when the scene can be captured with sufficient overlap and lighting consistency.
-
-### When Not to Use
-*   Avoid for scenes with extreme sparsity of views without additional denoising or regularization [S6].
-*   Avoid for scenes with significant transient occluders unless using methods like DeSplat [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Memory:** The memory footprint is dominated by the storage of Gaussian attributes. For $N$ Gaussians, the memory scales linearly with $N$. High-order spherical harmonics (SH Degree > 1) significantly increase memory usage [S3].
+*   **Time:** The rendering time is dominated by the sorting and rasterization steps. Sorting scales with $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with radix sort, depending on the implementation [S16]. The rasterization step is $O(P \cdot K)$, where $P$ is the number of pixels and $K$ is the average number of Gaussians per pixel [S16].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   GEMM-GS achieves an average 1.42× speedup over vanilla 3DGS implementation [S16].
+*   GEMM-GS provides an additional 1.47× speedup when combined with existing acceleration approaches [S16].
+*   LS-Gaussian achieves 5.41× speedup over the edge GPU baseline on average and up to 17.3× speedup with a customized accelerator [S8].
+
+**Scaling:**
+*   The method scales to large scenes with millions of Gaussians, but requires efficient data structures and hardware acceleration (e.g., Tensor Cores) to maintain real-time performance [S16].
+*   Distributed training and sharding allow scaling to even larger scenes by distributing the workload across multiple GPUs [S2].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering quality, explicit representation, ease of integration with existing pipelines [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes, sensitivity to initialization and hyperparameters (e.g., scene extent, densification thresholds) [S4][S9, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   Use when real-time novel view synthesis is required and the scene can be represented by a set of Gaussians [S9, unofficial].
+*   Use when hardware acceleration (CUDA, Tensor Cores) is available [S16].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+*   Avoid when memory is severely constrained and compression methods are not sufficient [S16].
+*   Avoid when the scene has complex, non-rigid deformations that are not well-modeled by static Gaussians [S15].
 
 ## Background
 
-### Origin
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. It represents a scene as a set of 3D Gaussians and uses differentiable rasterization for training and rendering.
-
-### History and Development
-Since its introduction, numerous methods have been developed to improve its efficiency, fidelity, and applicability, including acceleration techniques (GEMM-GS), densification schedules (DashGaussian), and extensions to dynamic scenes (SpacetimeGaussians) [S16, S13, S15898-spacetimegaussians-training-pipeline.md].
-
-### Where it is Used
-3DGS is used in computer vision, graphics, and robotics for novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and augmented reality [S9, unofficial][S15].
